ef 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1031 "The Greatest Common Factor of Two Monomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1033 2181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "507c4552-c3af-55c9-9104-67d8bb5a0bd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174918 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174918 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174918 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186174920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174918 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e57dad43-30f5-5af5-b15c-170042285046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174919 #edb/ref 1080 "-14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92a52351-b0ea-5214-b447-eb8bfc25cede" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174920 #edb/ref 1080 "-10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174920 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174920 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c44caa58-b68e-5fc5-ae37-46ffc70f9415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174921 #edb/ref 1080 "-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174921 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174921 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e5830896-5bea-546f-8950-0461dcc18f40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174922 #edb/ref 1080 "-9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174922 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174922 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e88abefb-6bda-5f3c-9741-77dfe56290a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174923 #edb/ref 1080 "-12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174923 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174923 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ddace94-63fa-5071-994a-652bc14cb466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175725 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175725 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor $3x^2 - 21x + 30.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175725 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ed3f2e8-2d0e-59e0-a9dc-1856245b768c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175725 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can factor out a $3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-21x+30 & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-7x+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial $x^2-7x+10$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(10),$ but only the negative factor pairs since the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is negative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1,-10),\\,(-2,-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-10) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-5) & =-7 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-7x=-2x-5x,$ and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x+10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-5x+10 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(-5x+10) & = \\\\ x(x-2)-5(x-2) & = \\\\ (x-5)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n3\\left(x^2-7x+10\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n3(x-5)(x-2)\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175797 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175797 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $-2x^2-22x-48.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175797 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c852599-e08e-5b54-be2b-700c3f8f47f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175797 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can factor out a $-2$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-22x-48 & = \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+11x+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial $x^2+11x+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,24),\\,(2,12),\\,(3,8),\\,(4,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(11)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+24 & =25 & \\times  \\\\ 2+12 & =14 & \\times  \\\\ 3+8 & =11 & ✓ \\\\ 4+6 & =10 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $11x=3x+8x,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n-2\\left(x^2+11x+24\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\left(x^2+3x+8x+24\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\left((x^2+3x)+(8x+24)\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\Bigl(x(x+3)+8(x+3)\\Bigr)&=\\[5pt]\n-2(x+8)(x+3)\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1039 "Factoring a Trinomial With a Negative Quadratic Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2d960485-d99c-555f-abb6-8e4d0564913e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176064 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176064 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176064 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176064 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176064 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176064 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176064 #edb/ref 1039 "Factoring a Trinomial Whose Terms Have a Common Factor" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176064 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176064 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "41472e46-65d2-579b-85b7-309749ed3a5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176126 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176126 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176126 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176126 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176126 #edb/ref 1045 "Factoring Trinomials Using Common Factors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176126 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176126 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176126 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176126 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4afc24dd-0109-5622-989e-7e2ca31e311d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176463 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176463 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32105" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176463 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176463 #edb/ref 1071 "If $-3{a}^{2}+30a-72=-3(a+p)(a+q),$ then $p+q=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176463 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c398936-3982-5ba0-b353-c27acab63b55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176463 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can factor out a $-3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}^{2}+30a-72 & =-3({a}^{2}-10a+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${a}^{2}-10a+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24).$ We will want to find the factor pair that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10),$ so we will consider the negative factor pairs in particular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & \\times (-24) \\\\ (-2) & \\times (-12) \\\\ (-3) & \\times (-8) \\\\ (-4) & \\times (-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-24) & =-25 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-12) & =-14 & \\times  \\\\ (-3)+(-8) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-4)+(-6) & =-10 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $-10a=-4a-6a,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({a}^{2}-10a+24) & = \\\\ -3({a}^{2}-4a-6a+24) & = \\\\ -3(a(a-4)-6(a-4)) & = \\\\ -3(a-4)(a-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-4,q=-6,$ and $p+q=-10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176675 #edb/ref 1061 25611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3a92aca-9db7-5c85-bff4-92c8a5b8ea08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176883 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176883 #edb/ref 1061 4262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1445b874-9aa3-5b17-b1f5-d21b64581d54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176918 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176918 #edb/ref 1061 5062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176918 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ed6467d-dfe0-510d-a23b-700569abb8d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176918 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176965 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to factor the polynomial \n\n$$\n8x^2 + 24x + 16.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks tricky because the coefficients are rather large! \n\nHowever, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $8.$ If we first factor out an $8$ from each of the terms, then we can cut the factoring down to something more manageable:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t8x^2 + 24x + 16 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t8(x^2 + 3x + 2)\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the polynomial $x^2 + 3x + 2$ in the parentheses.\n\nThe constant term $(2)$ has a single factor pair, namely\n\n$$\n(1, 2).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the numbers in this factor pair indeed sum to the linear term's coefficient $(1+2=3).$ \n\nSo, we can write the linear term as $3x=x+2x$ and factor as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+2 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+x+2x+2 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x)+(2x+2) & = \\\\ x(x+1)+2(x+1) & = \\\\ (x+2)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}+24x+16 & = \\\\ 8({x}^{2}+3x+2) & = \\\\ 8(x+2)(x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176965 #edb/ref 1065 1722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176965 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7336a5d6-0b38-5ba4-b278-aaac6a402ece" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176965 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499659 #edb/ref 1080 "-3(x+2)(x-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499659 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499659 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0472e445-7f02-503d-8b15-9cb91772ed43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499791 #edb/ref 1080 "(5t-4)(t+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499791 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499791 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "492942ee-22f4-59db-934a-e22733b6184d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499811 #edb/ref 1080 "5(t+2)(t-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499811 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499811 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52b70ec8-bf60-5ca2-9500-bf2667045ba3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499830 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499830 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499830 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b107508-c497-5022-876e-eecbf2e11f49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499886 #edb/ref 1080 "-3(x-2)(x+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499886 #edb/ref 1082 #e